@flexiarg operator/recognise-repeated-advice-by-recalling-where-it-was-given-before
@title Say That New Advice Seems Familiar, Name Where Similar Advice Was Given Before, And Note How It Differs
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-cjTuLj turn-jmtFzE
@parent peeragogy/discerning-a-pattern
@context An agent gives advice to a worker, and the operator has seen similar advice given to the same worker on a different project.

! conclusion: Say it seems familiar, name the earlier case, note how the new version differs, and use the repeat as the case for writing it down.

  + IF:
    The advice is the same in kind though more sophisticated.

  + HOWEVER:
    Treating each piece of advice as new hides that it recurs.

  + THEN:
    Say it seems familiar, name the earlier case, note how the new version differs, and use the repeat as the case for writing it down.

  + BECAUSE:
    Recalling the earlier instance is the first step in noticing a repetition and the second is naming it.
